finding: 1. src/cli/publish.ts:99–114,540–585 and src/cli/publish-fly.ts:160–166,259–274 — Recovery remembers only a mutable name, not the app created. After a failed publish with a provisioned volume, rename that Fly app without deleting its storage. The next retry sees the old name absent, creates a new app under it, and provisions a new volume while the original still bills. If the old name is instead reused by another accessible app inside the envelope, the pending name alone authorizes mutating that replacement (including initial provisioning on an empty replacement); no explicit hand-linked publish.server authorization is needed. Successful apps create precedes the initial save and does not itself claim a pre-existing app, but the saved record cannot establish identity on retry. 2. src/cli/publish-fly.ts:174–185,229–285 — Two retries of the same pending app with no Machines/volumes can both pass preflight and enter fly deploy. Pinned flyctl v0.5.12 machines.go:321 provisions before release creation, and deploy_first.go:131–185 consults a per-deploy in-memory volume inventory before creating. Both deploys can take the empty inventory before either creates a volume; neither Shallot nor that path serializes provisioning. Machine leases later in deployment do not guard this zero-Machine phase. That interleaving can leave two billable volumes even if one deployment later fails. The fake retry test runs sequentially and cannot catch this.
